# CODEOWNERS — automatic review ownership
# ashford/ppm-receiver-automation · Risk tier: Critical · Company scope: Premier (PPM)
#
# Ashford GitHub Standards v1.5 §6 (required file) and §7.2 (review expectations).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW THIS FILE WORKS, AND THE ONE RULE THAT MUST NOT BE BROKEN
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Under §3.1, External Partner Developers may contribute and discuss but CANNOT
# satisfy a required pull-request approval. Conscious Creations is an external
# partner. Therefore:
#
#   The partner team must NEVER be the sole owner of any path.
#
# Every rule below routes to a Premier-side team. Adding the partner team as an
# additional owner is optional and does not change who can approve; leaving a path
# owned only by the partner team would silently create an unapprovable path.
#
# Critical tier requires TWO approvals, at least one from a technically qualified
# Company Developer in @ashford/ppm-developers. The author cannot be one of them.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A RULE NAMING A PATH THAT DOES NOT EXIST PROTECTS NOTHING
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Until 10 September 2026 every financial-logic and security rule in this file
# pointed at /src/receiver_automation/**, which has never existed in this
# repository. GitHub does not warn about this: the rules simply never matched, so
# the code they were written to protect was covered only by the catch-all `*` and
# received no Platform Admin review at all.
#
# The paths below were verified to exist on 10 September 2026. If a module is
# moved or renamed, this file moves with it in the SAME commit.
#
# Two rules deliberately name paths that do not exist yet — /.github/workflows/
# and /docs/decisions/. That is correct and protective: a CODEOWNERS rule matches
# the files in a pull request, so these bind the moment either directory is
# created, which is exactly when review is most needed.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TEAM SLUGS REQUIRE PLATFORM ADMIN CONFIRMATION BEFORE MERGE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The slugs below follow the naming in Standards §3.1. Confirm each one exists and
# is spelled correctly with Ayotunde Gibbs or Henry Noel — GitHub silently ignores
# a CODEOWNERS rule that names a team which does not exist, which would leave the
# path with no owner at all.
#
#   @ashford/ppm-developers            Premier Company Developers — can approve
#   @ashford/ppm-production-approvers  Production deployment authority
#   @ashford/platform-admins           GitHub Platform Administrators
#
# The partner team slug is NOT yet confirmed. §3.1 gives "partner-fpt" only as an
# example of the pattern. Do not guess it — confirm the real slug, then uncomment
# the partner co-ownership lines at the bottom if desired.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


